{
  "description": "Weekdays with no scheduled SOFR publication, 2018-01-01 to 2030-12-31: the days `data.quarter_end` does not count as business days, besides Saturdays and Sundays. The panel's target is SOFR - IORB, so a business day for the calendar columns is a day the New York Fed publishes SOFR, and this table is that schedule, known in advance (#59, decided under Eleonora's delegation, 1 October 2026; the table itself follows her ruling of 1 October 2026 on #44, option A). The New York Fed publishes SOFR every business day, including days SIFMA recommends an early close, but not on days SIFMA recommends a full close, and it may, with advance notice, not publish on a day the Treasury repo market broadly treats as a holiday ('publication_source' on the rows that rest on that clause). An entry with basis 'statute' is a legal public holiday under 5 U.S.C. 6103(a) on its observed day (a Saturday holiday on the Friday before, a Sunday one on the Monday after); tests/test_data.py derives them from the statute and checks this list against the derivation. An entry with basis 'sifma' is a day beyond the statute, sourced to the SIFMA page that states SIFMA's recommendation; its reason records that recommendation, which is a full close except where it says early close. verified true means the row was read at its source on 1 October 2026 (#59). 'statutory_days_the_market_opened' lists observed statutory holidays on which SIFMA recommended no full close and SOFR was published; they are not in 'closed'. An unscheduled closure is a new entry, added by a reviewed pull request. data.MARKET_HOLIDAYS_SHA256 pins this file's sha256, and data.quarter_end refuses a file that does not match it, so a changed entry is a reviewed code change too. The table was extended from 2027-12-31 to 2030-12-31 on 7 October 2026 (#263, #228): the statutory rows for 2028-2030 follow from 5 U.S.C. 6103 and match the Federal Reserve Board's table K.8, 'Holidays Observed by the Federal Reserve System 2026-2030' (https://www.federalreserve.gov/aboutthefed/k8.htm, last updated 8 July 2026, read on 7 October 2026). SIFMA's recommendations stop at 2027 (https://www.sifma.org/resources/guides-playbooks/holiday-schedule, read on 7 October 2026), so the days that rest on one are in 'sifma_not_yet_published' and not in 'closed': Good Friday 2028-2030, and 2028-11-10, the Friday observed for Veterans Day on a Saturday. data.quarter_end and the business-day counts read them as open weekdays until SIFMA publishes; a test refuses a table on which any of them is within 30 days, and another refuses a table with less than 180 days left.",
